From 7dcaa2112c72ddc30facb34599edb0f56729f1a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 14:25:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] Repaint only the keys that changed on a shift or layer
 switch

Every shift tap, unlatch after a key and layer switch redrew the whole
keyboard: a full-surface fill plus every key, and damage for all of it. On
a 240x320 panel with a CPU blend in the compositor that is the most
expensive thing a typist triggers, and it repeats for every letter while
shift is latched.

The keyboard now remembers what the surface shows (layout, modifiers,
compose state). kbd_redraw_layout compares that with the wanted state key
by key and repaints only the keys whose label, highlight or colour scheme
differ, so drwsurf_damage and the back-buffer backport region cover just
those rectangles. A key still highlighted from a press is repainted too,
as a full draw would paint it. Toggling a modifier or compose also goes
through the diff, so every key carrying that modifier changes look, not
only the one that was touched; a layer with two Ctrl keys used to
highlight one.

The diff is only trusted when both layers put the same kind of key at the
same place. Different geometry, nothing drawn yet, or a surface that could
not be drawn on fall back to the full draw, which is also what resize and
show after hide still use. map_surfaces now runs the diff first, since
redimension_keyboard can switch the layer while the surface is unmapped
without drawing it.

Toggling a modifier off no longer draws the key preview popup for the
released key, since it goes through the same path shift always did.

Key rows can end one pixel past the surface edge when the width does not
divide evenly (seen at 200 px); that is existing behaviour and only
affects damage outside the surface.

test-damage renders a layer without a compositor, changes shift or the
layer, and checks that the damage and backport regions equal the union of
the rectangles of the keys that differ (computed from the layout tables,
not from the code under test), that every pixel outside the damage is
unchanged, and that the result equals a from-scratch render. It covers all
layer pairs that share a keymap, a synthetic scheme-only difference,
pressed keys, Ctrl and compose, and the full-redraw cases (first draw,
resize, undrawn surface).
Upstream-Status: Pending
